<article> <header class="hero"> <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"> <a href="/">Home</a> <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="9 18 15 12 9 6"/></svg> <a href="/glossary">Glossary</a> <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="9 18 15 12 9 6"/></svg> <span>HVPS+</span> </nav> <div class="hero-badge"><span class="pulse-dot"></span><span style="position:relative;z-index:1">Glossary · Schemes & Rules</span></div> <h1><span class="accent">HVPS+</span></h1> <p class="page-meta">Glossary entry · Last reviewed 26 July 2026</p> <!-- ANSWER BLOCK — picked up by speakable schema --> <p class="answer-block"><strong>HVPS+ (High-Value Payments Plus)</strong> is the market practice group and usage guideline defining a common ISO 20022 profile for the world’s major RTGS systems — T2, Fedwire, CHAPS, BoJ-Net, RITS, Lynx and others. At the BIS PIE taskforce, Fostering ISO 20022 harmonisation snapshot its status is <strong>"Scheduled"</strong>: hybrid is implemented, with unstructured removal set for the November 2026 cutover.</p> </header> <section class="section"> <div class="section-label">In Practice</div> <h2 class="section-title">How HVPS+ works in practice</h2> <div class="content-block"><p>The Scheduled→Aligned transition at the cutover is why transitional handling (PART XIII) exists: once HVPS+ removes fully unstructured addresses, in-flight pre-cutover transactions returning through post-cutover infrastructure need Best-Effort Structuring or, exceptionally, the CUTOVER2026 keyword. Decommissioning is synchronised with CBPR+ to prevent fragmentation across channels.</p></div> <p class="src">Source: BIS PIE taskforce, Fostering ISO 20022 harmonisation §3.1.1 / pp.11-8 to 11-9; the ioNova Postal Address Specification v2.0 PART XIV §42.1 / §43.2; §10.7.</p> <div class="content-block"><p>Parent reference: <a href="/address-intelligence/structured-address-mandate">ISO 20022 structured address mandate</a>.</p></div> </section> <section class="section"> <div class="section-label">Citations</div> <h2 class="section-title">Sources</h2> <div class="sources-list"> <h3 class="sources-subhead">Standards &amp; regulatory sources</h3>
